e wind estimate is still a little short on eastward push, so I'm considering stronger wind at altitude or more shear, and the 150 m test is the one I'm relying on most. The measured 100 m wind of 6.7 \u00b1 0.5 isn't enough on its own, so I need more data. Seven tests remain, and I plan to do the smoke drops next to check for updrafts, releasing at x = -90, y = 0 on heading 90, before moving on to high-speed, low-altitude, and different-heading drops.\n\nI'm planning the smoke drop at 100 m altitude with speed 50, and also checking a lower release near 40 m, since the updraft effect scales with altitude up to 15 m."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "The measured fall time of 9.05 is well above the roughly 7.8 I'd expect without an updraft, so there's clearly a significant upward current. I'll set that against my earlier guess of 3 for the updraft."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "I need more updraft, so I'll try raising it to 4 from the current 3, since the current delta is at -0.35."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "I'm treating the updraft as roughly 3.9, though it depends on where the smoke path of the drop sits, and hotspots vary in intensity, so the model scales updraft by intensity. Next I want to test veer and wind at 30 m and 150 m altitude across different headings and speeds, including a high-speed case at 150 m heading 180 and a low-altitude case at 30 m heading 270.\n\nI'm settling on three more drops to check veer and wind: one heading 180 at 150 m with a speed of 80, one heading 270 at 30 m with a speed of 45 released from x=300, y=-300, and one heading 90 at 130 m with a speed of 60 from x=-400, y=300, all kept away from the smoke."}, {"before_call": 13, "text": "I'm checking the wind readings at each height: about 5.7 at 30, 5.5 at 50, 6.7 to 6.9 at 100, and 7.9 to 8.3 at 150. Taking 6.8 at 100 and applying a shear exponent of 0.3 gives about 7.7 at 150, but extrapolating down to 30 gives 4.7, which is lower than the measured 5.7. The data is noisy, so I want to compare shear exponents of 0.3 and 0.25 against it."}, {"before_call": 14, "text": "The high-altitude drops are landing negative in x, which points to more wind aloft than the model assumes. I'll try raising the wind speed to 7.0 and the shear to 0.35, with direction at 91. The low-altitude test 6 already looks fine at dt -0.12, so I'll leave that one alone."}, {"before_call": 15, "text": "I'm checking the test drops: most of the residuals are within noise, and the heading-180 high-speed case suggests the drag term is what's driving the offset. I'm considering a slightly tighter drag value and a few more drops, since the hotspot precision needs to be better than the roughly 2 m errors I'm seeing.\n\nI'm going through the veer and updraft offset: the direction readings at 150 m and 30 m look about the same, so veer seems close to zero. I'm planning four more drops, starting with a low-altitude release at 40 m, speed 45, off-center at x=25, to test updraft, and then a 70 m drop on heading 225."}, {"before_call": 17, "text": "Test 7 came up short, with y at -89.6 rather than reaching smoke. I'll set that aside and compare it against the others."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "I'm checking which tests actually put the smoke in the updraft's path. Only test 4 clearly did, since test 7's path stays well outside the smoke. I want a few more drops for refinement, so I'm planning an off-axis smoke pass at altitude 60, heading 90, speed 45, offset 20 in y, and working out the travel distance.\n\nI'm estimating the travel distance for a drop at altitude 60 and speed 45, landing around 85, and I'm setting the release point at x=-80, y=20. I'm also noting a possible follow-up drop at altitude 30 through the center."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "Test 9 gave a time step of 0.14 and test 4 gave 0.06, and the predicted fall came out longer than it should be, so the updraft may be a little too strong. I'll try an updraft of 3.7, drag of 0.038, and wind speed of 7.2."}, {"before_call": 21, "text": "I'm comparing test 9's dt, which is still +0.14 even with the updraft going from 4 to 3.7. That seems small, so I'm wondering whether to try updraft 4.5 to see how test 9 responds, since the earlier 3\u21924 change moved test 4 by 0.4. The fact that test 9 didn't move at all is odd, and some other change may be affecting it.\n\nI'm going to try drag 0.037, wind speed 7.1, and updraft 4.5 together."}, {"before_call": 22, "text": "Raising the updraft makes the predicted time longer, so a positive dt means the prediction is running too long and the updraft should come down. Updraft 4 gave residuals of +0.06 on test4 and +0.14 on test9, so I'll try 3.5 with drag at 0.037 and ws at 7.1."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "I'm treating the updraft estimate of about 3.8 as good enough. Test 7 still shows an x-offset of -3.3 at low altitude heading north, which I'm attributing possibly to wind direction or veer, given the noise of about 1.5. I'm weighing whether to spend the last test drop now or hold it for a later configuration, and I'm leaning toward running one more test at altitude 30 first.\n\nI'm weighing whether to use the last test drop on a high-speed run at altitude 35, heading south, with the release offset to compensate for the expected drift, though the travel distance is uncertain and the risk of missing makes me lean toward skipping it and just dropping at the fixed point instead."}, {"before_call": 25, "text": "I'm settling on a working hypothesis with drag at 0.037, wind speed 7.1, direction 91, shear 0.35, no veer, and an updraft of 3.8, and I'm starting the operation on that basis."}, {"before_call": 26, "text": "I'm looking at how the simulate tool handles the fire: it seems to model only a single fire at the origin with intensity 1, so the updraft is defined around that point. Since the targets have different intensities and fire positions, I'm considering shifting coordinates to reuse that model, and for the no-smoke case, running it with zero updraft.\n\nI'm weighing whether the smoke column can be handled by simulate with an adjusted updraft, since the radius and peak scale with intensity and the tool can't match both at once. Avoiding the smoke would be ideal, but the load falls through the column when aimed at the center, so its updraft, fall rate, and drift all need accounting for. I'll start with an updraft of 3.8 times the intensity and refine from there.\n\nI'm estimating how the updraft's effect scales with intensity, since the fire's radius and peak both change and simulate can't match them both. Rough scaling suggests an updraft of about 3.8 \u00d7 I \u00d7 \u221aI should approximate the exposure time in the column, so I'll try that as a first pass.\n\nI'm settling on a low altitude to limit how far wind errors carry the load, and I'll use the updraft value of 3.8 \u00d7 I^1.5 I worked out before. For the heading, I'll pick any value in the allowed range, since the approach path can be aligned to suit it.\n\nI'm settling on how to pick the release point: I'll set the heading, then work out the release offset by running the simulation with the fire at the origin and translating the result to the target's position. For the first target I'm trying a heading of 84 degrees, an altitude of 45, a speed of 67, and an updraft of about 4.3 from the intensity of 1.09.\n\nDrag looks strong enough that horizontal speed should die off within a second, leaving the release dominated by wind and the stopping distance. My earlier test runs put travel somewhere between roughly 35 and 56 meters, so I'll start the release offset at about 60 meters.\n\nI'm settling on a first test release point about 60 meters upwind of the target, simulating from there at heading 84."}, {"before_call": 31, "text": "The fall at altitude 45 takes about 5.2 seconds because of the updraft, so the travel distance is larger than I'd expected. The second target, aimed at heading 239 from (60, 35), came down at (60, 5.1), which